For each required name, find its result. Keep the name in the blocked list if it is missing, not successful, or belongs to another revision.

results.find(item => item.name === wantedName) returns the first matching result, or undefined when none matches. Check for that missing value before reading its status or revision. filter keeps each required name for which your blocking condition is true.

```javascript
const results = [{name:"tests",status:"success"}];
console.log(results.find(item => item.name === "tests").status);
console.log(results.find(item => item.name === "build"));
console.log(["tests", "build"].filter(name => name === "build"));
```
The outputs are success, undefined, and ["build"]. find returns the first matching element or undefined; filter returns a new array of all matching elements. Each receives a predicate callback and leaves the source array unchanged. These calls show selection only; the full exercise also checks revision and status.

